yeah great thank you i'm happy to be  here uh greetings from boston wish i  could be there in person  um as you see i'm there in spirit maybe  next year i'll manage to actually make  it over  um i'll share my screen to get started  one second to pull up my slides i'm  impressed by the decoration by the way  on point  thank you put some real thought into it  today  so um as mentioned i'm andrew terrio  i am a data science educator uh  consultant  um i think you know most notably for the  topic we're gonna get into today i used  to be the chief data officer of the city  of boston  for about two years i ran the city's  analytics team and you know was involved  in a lot of open data projects not only  with boston but with collaborations with  other cities  all around the world i also have spent  quite a few years working  in the political sector um i was at the  democratic national committee which is  in the u.s the  democratic party headquarters um i'm  also the co-organizer of the pi data  boston chapter so  uh you know this group is really near  and dear to me so  today what i want to talk about is the  intersection of civic data science  and open data we'll get into a bit  in a moment about what that means but i  want to talk about those in particular  because  as nicholas mentioned you know there's  often a challenge of  finding projects in the data science and  open source community  that really make great use of open data  and are long lasting and have a really  substantial impact  as i'll talk about you know we see a lot  of work being done with open data and  using open source tools  um but the number of those that actually  make it all the way to having  a major real-world impact is actually  pretty small and i think i've got a few  ideas why and i want to share them with  you today  so we're going to talk about what those  challenges are and i want to provide  a sense for everybody watching of you  know what it means to work with this  kind of data  how to design projects that are  effective and are successful at reaching  their goals  how to build partnerships with you know  governments and non-profits and others  that you need to really have that kind  of impact  and how to make the projects pay off  over the long term how to think  sustainably about you know what the  model  beyond just the initial release looks  like in order to  make sure that you actually have a  substantial impact  so just to get started i want to talk  about some sort of basics of what we're  discussing today  so this is the definition that i use for  civic data science it's the application  of data science methods  to support the work of governments  nonprofits advocacy groups political  campaigns  and other organizations which exist to  deliver positive social impact  and there are two things i like to focus  on here one is the idea of data science  methods i define that  really broadly so you know that's  everything from your basics of data  management and analysis to  all the way to predictive modeling ai  machine learning  you know all of the fancy tools that get  a lot of press i think the entire  spectrum really matters  um and you know i define data science  pretty broadly to just say  it's the scientific use of data to  accomplish a real world goal  the other part that i think is important  here is the last bit about  these organizations existing to deliver  positive social impacts  um i think it's an important distinction  to make when you compare it to the  commercial sector where  you know the goal of an organization is  to make money and maybe they have a  positive  social impact as well but ultimately the  motivations matter  and the reason i think civic data  science is important  number one is that i think these  organizations are really important  um because that is their main goal that  it's not just a byproduct of trying to  make money  that you know the entire focus of these  organizations is to promote  civic good and and i should note too um  you know when i talk about  social good or civic good um that is a  subjective definition what i consider  good is not necessarily the same thing  that one of you might  but you know overall the idea is uh  simply that  you're doing things for a reason other  than you know to make money  and and i think this is something where  the open source community is really tied  into i mean  when you're developing open source  software you know you're doing it to  enable  others to make use of it uh above and  beyond what you  you know yourself might actually make of  it and  that's something that's done for a  social good it's not just  being done for your own commercial gain  that's the opposite of open source  software  but i think what's really important  about civic data science particularly  is that there's a huge potential for  impact and the reason for that is simply  that  you know these kinds of organizations  tend to be uh  constrained in resources and that's  really the circumstance where data  science  is most vital and has the biggest impact  because through making things  more effective and efficient you're able  to really  help overcome some of those resource  challenges that those organizations face  but the other thing too i want to point  out is it's you know not just about  doing this sort of moral good in the  world um working on these kind of  projects is actually really exciting  um they're often fun to work on the  people you work with are really great  and there's a lot of opportunities to do  creative and innovative things  and you know all in all if you have a  chance to do it i'd highly recommend it  so open data well what is open data  you know there are many definitions you  could point to i think the key  factors that you should think about when  you're thinking about open data is that  it's data that's published in a public  forum that's available to everybody  and can be used for any purpose that  there's no restrictions on  what you can and can't use a given data  set for and ideally it's published in  some kind of you know machine  readable easy to use format um you know  the point there is once somebody  publishes it it's out there for anybody  to use for any purpose  and when we talk about it today i'm  mainly going to be focusing on  government published data  that's what i have a lot of experience  with and that's what most people think  of when they think of open data but  there are other sources as well  that also fall within this category  so when we're talking about government  data um there are a few different areas  that this comes into um one is  government research  so you know maybe the most classic open  data set that honestly i didn't even  really think of as open data until i  started preparing this talk  uh in the us is the census you know the  census bureau  creates these huge data sets of all  sorts of demographic characteristics of  the population  uh likewise there's a lot of public data  sets about economics  um there's a lot you know in the news  these days about public health with the  cobit 19 crisis  under other things like weather data  sets that you might not even think of as  open data  but really that's simply because they're  so essential to  you know a lot of what people rely on  every day  and then there's data about public  services that are provided it might be  police or fire or you know emergency  medical services  it might be things like education or  health care or housing  all the things that a government does to  provide services for community  you know those generally create data and  open data programs often publish that  data  so others can can use it for whatever  purposes  they see fit there's also internal  government operational data so  that'll be things like budgets or  expenditures when i work for the city of  boston uh it was an interesting time  because  you know my salary and everyone else in  in city hall you know all of our our  information about that was public record  it was published in the community  you know when i when my job was  announced um there was a  article in the boston globe our local  newspaper  that listed you know my background my  experience my title and how much money  i'm going to be  making um all that stuff is available  there's also a lot of information about  properties and this is one thing that  i think a lot of people don't quite  recognize that government is the  maintainer of but  everything about property ownership uh  valuation  transactions um and obviously in some  countries maybe this is  different um and i'm mostly speaking to  the us here but i  you know from what i've seen in a lot of  places it's similar that this is all  maintained by the government and made  available  through either open data programs  explicitly or  through um different government programs  that  then pass it on to other organizations  and then finally a key part of  open data is geospatial data you know  everything you see on a map  that you would think of as you know  street names addresses all of that is  maintained by some government agency  that's responsible for coordinating  um that kind of information and so you  know at the end of the day you might  pull something up on google maps or open  street map or  somewhere like that but you know  generally that originated with some  government planning office that decided  you know this street has this name from  this place to this place  and so all of those kinds of things as  well as all the characteristics of  things  uh around a city those are available in  geospatial data sets  so just to give a really brief uh  overview of what  an open data program looks like this is  analyze boston  it's the open data portal for the city  of boston it's actually the city's  second open data portal we released it  about a year into my time there and you  know we really  overhauled the entire program if you  dive into this site  you'll find currently as of at least  last monday they've got about 160  data sets published and many of these  update every day  as you can see here you know there's a  whole number of these that  they update on a daily basis with the  latest data they sync directly with  internal systems  you know just to pull up one of these  data sets about um electricity usage  at the central library in the city you  know this is something that's recorded  by  an internal uh electricity meter  that's stored on city computers and then  every four hours there's a script run by  my old team  that grabs that data from whatever  server it lives on  and pushes it up to our open data portal  so if you want to build some kind of  real-time monitor yourself  you'd be able to do that um as a  developer  you know anywhere in the world so what  this looks like in practice  is you know this is really raw data in  this case it's simply  a date time and the amount of of  kilowatts being drawn  at that particular point in time by the  building and  you know you have the ability to view it  online you can download a csv  or some other format or you can query an  api if you want to pull it  pull the data to build it into your own  application  so just to give a few brief examples of  what this often looks like in practice  when people use this data so this is a  project that's near and dear to my heart  it actually started out as something  that  originated from my team at city hall um  it was an idea that we had based on our  work on  our city's vision zero program which was  all about  uh you know reducing uh crash  fatalities and injuries to zero on our  streets  and we decided that you know this was  something that was broader than boston  and we wanted to build this on a sort of  open source model  so we actually partnered with a um sadly  now more or less defunct volunteer  organization called data for democracy  and this became an open source project  essentially what it does is it takes  things like crash histories around the  city  combines it with features of the various  streets  and develops a predictive model to say  what is the risk on any given street  segment  and this can be used by traffic planning  departments to  figure out what kind of interventions  you can take to minimize those risks  it's currently available for six  different cities and the team's working  to build more  you can find more information about that  at insightlane.org if you're interested  another project that came out of chicago  was about using  open data on e coli measurements at the  city's beaches to build a model to  predict where there is likely to be  high levels of of hazard at these  beaches and so  this was a collaboration with a  community developer group  where they basically built an open  source model to improve forecasts for  the beaches where they're not able to  get a recent measurement  to figure out well where should they be  paying attention to you know they didn't  have the resources to measure at every  beach every day  so they would take the measurements they  did get and use that to predict where  they should focus  next in order to improve the quality or  improve their uh knowledge about the  water quality at all these sites so that  they could  protect the public's health another  project  sadly the illustration isn't that uh  helpful right now because there's no  snow on the ground  but in syracuse they built an app an  open source tool  to take real-time information about this  the snow plowing throughout the city  and display that on a website so that  you know citizens could see where  um you know where the snowplows were  when their street was going to get  plowed um that saved a lot of you know  time and energy  for them to reach out to city hall  directly to try to ask those questions  um this is something that you know a lot  of commercial providers would make  available  but at a pretty substantial cost and  they were able to do it with open source  software  and make it freely available and this is  a project from  a a non-government group it's um an  advocacy group in san diego where they  took  government data from the census and from  state  nutrition programs and mapped where  childhood poverty was concentrated  throughout san diego county in  california  and they use that to provide an insight  for for anyone who wants to look at this  to see  you know where is poverty concentrated  and where did they need to really put  some work in  and then finally an example um that i  mentioned kind of earlier that  you know anytime you pull up a weather  app if you're pulling up pulling out  your phone you're getting information  from google or apple or whatever um you  know those organizations generally  aren't collecting weather data and  producing their own forecast  they're taking government forecasts from  places like the national weather service  in the us  or you know similar organizations in  europe and elsewhere  and so that kind of information it's not  something you typically think of as open  data but it's really a vital commodity  but so it's actually a challenge to come  up with even just five examples of this  um  because you know for the thousands and  thousands of projects that i see  produced every year  uh very few of them actually get to that  point where you have something  that's worth pointing out in a  conference presentation  and there's a few reasons why for that  and you know these  are uh problems that i think any data  scientist will see is not unique  to civic data science but is  particularly important here because some  of the unique  challenges so problem one that often  comes up is  just that people sometimes have a poor  understanding of the data or the subject  matter  or the stakeholders or users involved um  you know data scientists are notorious  for  jumping into new fields head first and  saying you know i know all this stuff  about data therefore i can solve your  fields problems  without really understanding the context  and likewise the second problem that  happens is sometimes things get built  without  what's called a theory of change you  know that is some mechanism  by which whatever's getting built will  translate into some  real world impact um often times people  think well if i do an analysis or i  build a  map or an app or a model or whatever  change will just happen afterwards  without really thinking through  what that mechanism looks like and as  part of that  the third problem comes up which is that  there's often a failure to connect  with those potential end users who can  actually build something  or do something with whatever you're  producing so for example in the case of  that  that crash prediction model you know the  end users  would be these uh transportation  departments in cities or states or  national governments who might actually  be in a place to make changes  that would say solve problems on  high-risk street segments  if you're never getting your  deliverables to those people  it's hard to see what that mechanism is  for you know actually having the impact  you're looking for  also with some of these projects you  have an over-reliance on  single individuals or small groups  who are generally volunteers and you  know not getting paid to do this and so  their availability is limited and even  if they're really enthusiastic and  committed at first  you know it's hard to sustain that over  time there's also  along the same lines you know often a  lack of quality control or refinement  where a lot of these projects make it to  the mvp stage  to the sort of minimum viable product  but they don't actually get refined to  the  level that you know they would in some  kind of commercial application and so  um you know be almost there but not  quite ready for for actual use in the  real world  and then finally after something is  built oftentimes these projects  um you get to that mvp stage but nobody  thinks about what comes next how do you  deploy it  how do you support it and maintain it  going forward and that means a lot of  these projects they get released they  get some press  and then they just kind of go away and  being able to deal with all these  problems  is really what differentiates good  projects from those who just  that we see all the time coming out of  class projects or hackathons  that never really go anywhere after the  initial development  so um you know one of the things i do  when i teach my students  data science is to really emphasize the  importance of  project design and what i mean by design  is the part of a project you do before  you ever dive into data  that you know i'd like to think of it's  like a pen and paper exercise  you know it's not something you're doing  with code uh it's really the part where  you think  through your project ahead of time and  what you're trying to accomplish here  is to think about you know what is the  purpose of your project  what is the scope of it you know what  are the things you want to do what are  the things that are outside of that  scope and and what's the final  output you also think about things like  who are the stakeholders and users  and you think about the requirements for  success and and what risks  could come up um this is often used as  an opportunity to get feedback from  whether it's your potential users  or you know some partners you're working  with or even just other data scientists  before development starts so you don't  spend a lot of time doing something  that has a foreseeable problem and it  also when you're working with a team  gives you  a shared vision and roadmap that you can  then use to drive the rest of the  development  and like i said this is important to all  kinds of data science projects  but it's particularly key for these  civic data science projects because  you know oftentimes if they start going  off in the wrong direction  they'll lose steam and never get  finished and a lot of these  issues that come up are often  preventable so  i put together 10 questions here that  are are worth asking when you're coming  up with a project design so  first one is simply what is the overall  goal you're working towards and what i  mean is  what's the real world problem you're  trying to solve you know for example in  the traffic  uh modeling or the crash modeling  application the real world goal  is to reduce the risk of crashes on  sadie streets  and then you ask who are the  stakeholders partners and users  sometimes those groups will overlap  sometimes they'll be distinct  but you need to identify them to be able  to really build to their needs  and then ask you know for these users  what do they want to know and and  how would that actually help them  accomplish that goal that you and that  you set out in the first question  it helps to remember that data science  provides value by providing information  that it helps others do their jobs  better so this is the point where you  really define  what is it what is it that would be  helpful for them to know  and then number four to get there what  inputs do you need  um and how do you get them so what are  your original data sources you know what  are the things that are available  but don't quite answer the question or  provide the information  that the users need in its current form  so then five  what follows is how do you transform  that data to get there and what does the  end result tell you  sometimes it'll be exactly what you want  in number three sometimes it will be  something slightly different or there'll  be caveats  and it's important to really understand  what it is you're producing  so then number six uh what kind of  resources do you need whether it's  time technical infrastructure support  from other people  or whatever what do you need to complete  the project  uh this is a point where you kind of do  a sanity check and say  does this make sense do i have  everything i need and what you might  find  here is well this is a good project idea  i could do it  but i need these resources that aren't  available and at that point you know  maybe you say well i'm gonna go look for  those and if i can't find them i'm not  gonna start and waste my time on  something that's not gonna eventually  succeed  so then seven this is also the time to  think about what the risks  are that could make your project fail um  it might be you know one of these  requirements isn't met  it might be that you know some  potentially  unforeseen circumstance comes up like  you know a data source that you're  counting on  suddenly goes away um in some cases that  might  you know lead you to try to mitigate  those risks in other cases  you know maybe there's nothing you can  do but at least you have a good  understanding of  well what's the likelihood of this  project succeeding or failing and does  that justify the resources needed to  complete it  number eight how do you validate the  quality of what you're producing  this is a really important thing whether  it's a model an analysis  an application or anything else because  not only do you want to make sure what  you're delivering is high quality  you want to know how you can use that  validation to build trust among these  potential end users  who many of whom probably aren't that  technical and might not instinctively  trust what you build the way you would  because you know how it  works and they don't then number nine  how are you going to provide those  results to the end users are you going  to build an application  will you be giving reports um what does  this  look like and what is what needs to come  with it in terms of training  and documentation and so forth and then  finally number 10  what happens after you've delivered um  because a project  you know is going to continue on in the  long term  and what does it take to make sure it  delivers value over time  so just two specific points i want to  delve  delve into before starting to wrap up um  one thing is about the importance of  partnerships  so it's critically important to make  sure that you find  people who know about whatever subject  area you're working in and ideally have  some ability to affect  change in that and and generally that  means people who aren't data scientists  they can help you understand the subject  matter understand your data  figure out what's actually a useful  research question to answer  a lot of times you know what data  scientists think is an interesting  finding or a useful  tool might not be for reasons that we  can't foresee because we're not on the  ground  and then also these are the people who  will put your results into practice and  so if you really want to have an impact  you need them on your side and finally  in the long term  in an ideal world it's really helpful to  try to get these people  to put the results or to put whatever  you build  into sort of long-term support on their  end rather than  requiring you to keep maintaining it  over time and so when you're working  with these partners i think  it helps to think through what their  incentives are what are their their  limitations  um and sometimes you might you know have  to look around for potential partners  where  you know maybe for example i want to  build something for boston  but the partner say in the  transportation department here  you know maybe they're too busy to help  or they're a little skeptical um rather  than just giving up that's a time to say  you know maybe i'll go over to cambridge  across the river and say  you know they might be more interested  or look at you know a smaller city even  um and that might be a way to get off  the ground where then  if you have success with them you can  come back later to boston and say hey  are you  interested now that there's something  more to show and then finally you know  be respectful of their time and  expertise  recognize their they may not be data  experts but they're experts in their own  field  and you know listen to their critiques  and feedback and be willing to change  your direction based on it  and then thinking through the long term  just remember your project's not done  when you think it's finished  you're going to need to provide support  to users provide ongoing infrastructure  there will be things that come up around  changes to data sources and  dependencies and so forth a good project  should also be monitored and quality  controlled over time  and if something goes wrong somebody's  going to need to fix it and at the end  of its life cycle  somebody's going to need to figure out  you know what happens when  you know just incremental changes aren't  enough to keep it producing value  and you know in the big picture i think  it's worth remembering that a poorly  supported project  is actually potentially a worse outcome  than nothing at all  for your partners and end users where  you know maybe what they're doing right  now isn't ideal  it's not the best possible solution they  could have but at the same time  you know maybe it's working for them and  if you were to give them some new tool  that then breaks later on that could  actually  leave them worse off than if they just  kept doing what they're doing and so  if at all possible too um the other  piece of advice i'd give  is it's ideal to have long-term support  provided by people who are  um actually getting paid to maintain it  rather than volunteers because obviously  they have an incentive um to make sure  that  you know this stays current and accurate  over time  so just to wrap up um a few final  thoughts i want to offer  uh the first is simply think about the  value proposition of data science and  you know i mentioned before that  it's really about providing information  to others to do their jobs better  um the flip side of that is you know  stay humble and remember that data  scientists  aren't generally the people who produce  anything themselves  you know they their job is to help other  people do their jobs better  and so that's really why these  partnerships really matter if you're  passionate about a specific area  i would really strongly advise you to  spend some time learning how it works  not just by diving into the data but  actually going and talking to people  reading books you know really starting  to understand the subject so you can be  an effective contributor  in terms of doing project designs um you  know i  proposed a lot of questions you should  ask it doesn't mean you have to know all  the answers to get started  but it does help to at least know you  don't know so you can have an idea in  mind of how you're going to get there  how you'll answer those questions  and as you go through a project you  should be open to  changing direction if you learn new  information or even  walking away if it's not working out the  way you planned  you know your time is valuable and in  some cases it may help to you know just  put your head down and try to power  through  obstacles but in some cases maybe not  maybe you learn something that says oh  this original idea i had  isn't really what's needed or you know  the data we have doesn't really  tell us what we thought it did and maybe  it's time to think about  you know how to spend your time uh in a  better way on some other project instead  you know thinking deliberately about  your projects helps you do that  and then finally if things do go well  you also want to think about how your  projects can scale  so you know i mentioned the the crash  modeling project where  we wanted to start it for boston then we  realized why not  do this as a general thing that's  available elsewhere  so any city with similar data can plug  theirs in and get these kind of outputs  that's really kind of the ethos of the  open source community after all  and so if your project goes well that's  a great time to think about how do you  generalize that to  other use cases so finally  all right hopefully i've sold you on the  the importance of these kind of projects  and  you know got you a little excited about  it so how do you get started well  there's a lot of different ways to get  started um you could start going through  open data sites on your own  you could talk to people who run an open  data program i would note that those  people tend to be pretty  busy and overworked and they may or may  not be available don't take it  personally if they  don't quite have time to hold your hand  through it but you can also get involved  with community groups  and work with say the people you know  who produce this data  in the different government agencies or  departments or programs  you could also connect with others in  your local pi data group or other groups  and sometimes you know depending on  where you are there'll be things like  hackathons and open data events  um you know i'm not a huge hackathon  person because  i worry sometimes that you know you're  doing the fun part of data science  but then just walking away before the  hard part comes  but it is a good way to get started and  i think the key  no matter which direction you go is just  remember to think deliberately  about what your projects you're building  and  plan for the long term think through  what it takes for it to be successful  and then you know commit to it enough to  follow through and  you know make sure it really pays off on  its full potential  so that's all i've got um i don't know  if i have questions time for questions  right now but i will be on discord  afterwards  um you can also reach me on twitter and  i've posted the slides  uh to github you can see the address hit  here so  if anything you want to go back and look  at please do  thank you thank you so much for your  talk and i'm really hoping that  this inspires some people to check out  these projects and  also give them the consideration that  they deserve to hopefully  go from purely hackathon to something  that's sustainable  um i would say if you're up for it  considering this is the very last talk  it's no issue if you run over a tiny bit  if you feel like  um having a look at the q a questions  that are currently being posted  maybe you feel like answering some of  them yeah so um the first one who comes  up with the projects in boston  um so this is a really interesting  challenge when i was at city hall where  um you know when i started what happened  generally was i had a team i came in  with a team about 20 people  and the the existing process was simply  that somebody in another department  would go to somebody on my team and say  hey can you do this and then they'd say  yes or no  and so sometimes that we ran into  problems where you know  um two people might be working on the  same thing without realizing it or  you know we just took on too many  projects because individually they all  sounded good  um but the problem was is that meant we  were over committed and weren't really  delivering  uh you know on the schedule we want to  or with the quality we wanted so what we  actually  did was put in place this analytics  project manager role there  where we had individuals on the team i  think there were there were three in  total  who were tasked with being the points of  contact to work with specific  departments  you know we had one that was working on  housing and healthcare and education  another that worked on you know  transportation and public works and  other city services  and a third we worked on performance  management and budgeting  and you know across those groups  they developed long-term relationships  with the partners and then helped them  scope out you know a portfolio of  projects that was more narrow  but was a better fit for actually  executing at the level of quality  and the timeliness that we wanted  uh another question who designs the  projects  eventually so um it really depends on  the particular project sometimes it will  be that project manager that i spoke of  will take the lead  and run through all of this and  generally you know they'll be  responsible for the general scoping and  planning of a project  but in some cases for example it might  be a purely geospatial  project and we had people on the team  who were gis experts  and in those cases you know it may just  get handed off to  those people who are specialists in that  particular area  to work directly with the partners to  plan out the project  um without much involvement from the  project manager so it really depended on  the project and the specific need  in terms of how to propose a different  way of setting up hackathons  actually a good example might be if you  were to go to the analyze boston site  that i showed earlier  it's uh the address of data.boston.gov  there's a showcases section in there to  provide examples of  work that was done with with boston's  open data and a lot of the content on  there  came from a challenge we did when we  released the new site  back in 2017. what we did is we had a  month-long  open data challenge program where when  we released the site  we uh essentially gave five different  areas  as we talked to other departments we had  you know the fire department the  environment department and a few others  that had ideas for what could be done  with the open data they were publishing  and so they put you know sort of calls  for projects out there  and then we basically gave we set up a  competition  where we gave teams um which who  in some cases were students in some  cases were community members or even  people at companies who had a little  extra time to work on things  um and and we gave we tasked them with  working on one of these five areas  and then a month later we did a full day  open data challenge showcase where we  basically had all the teams  give presentations um we had the  community  pick winners uh for various prizes uh  and overall that went really well i wish  that was something that  you know had been able to continue it's  just event organizing as  uh the organizers here know is a lot of  work um and  we weren't really set up to keep doing  that over time but what actually  was really great about that was by  giving teams the opportunity to  spend you know more than just say a day  working on a project but build it over  time and get feedback  um that made for a lot higher quality  projects  you know a lot more polished  it also gave an opportunity to connect  with the stakeholders so you know when  we when we had these presentations  we had people from the fire department  and the public schools and the  environment department  and all these others um at the events  and so that really provided a way for  them to see  you know what the point of publishing  this data in the first place could be  and  really understand the potential of the  things that could get built and some of  those things  um generally hackathon projects don't  turn into  you know long-term uh projects directly  but in a lot of cases you do see that  they get adopted  um or adapted you know by internal teams  and improved on from there so you know  we did that we certainly did that with  city hall quite a bit  um and then also at uh the syracuse  example that i showed earlier  um you know that was initially a  hackathon project that then  ended up getting brought in and they had  i believe it was a fellow on their team  um who was i think the summer fellow or  something um  you know took this and worked on it for  several months and built into a real  product  so that's often a great way to kind of  make that transition  and like i mentioned i am a big fan of  having these projects  uh eventually owned by people whose job  it is to maintain them over time  and so sometimes that's actually a  really good model for collaboration  awesome thank you so much that makes a  lot of sense  you
 

